  "narrative_body": "Almost all Poland coverage this week came from a single institution — the Institute of National Remembrance (IPN). The governing College nominated Mateusz Szpytma for the presidency. Under the statute, the nomination now goes to Karol Nawrocki, the president, who makes the formal appointment. Mr Szpytma is a historian and former vice-president of the institute; Mr Nawrocki ran it from 2021 to 2025 and shaped it to advance national-conservative historical narratives. The institute has a budget of roughly €88 million a year and more than 2,000 staff; its president shapes how Poland officially narrates Volhynia, the Holocaust, the Cursed Soldiers and the Round Table — all politically live issues. The appointment is not yet made, but the channel is open.\n\nThe week's other IPN events were less consequential but not unrelated. On April 19, the institute marked the 83rd anniversary of the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising — a scheduled event, but one that fell immediately after the previous week's Confederation swastika incident, in which party figures staged antisemitic parliamentary theatre on Yom HaShoah and the American ambassador rebuked them publicly. The commemoration proceeded without disruption. The institute also attended a Visegrád Four (V4) conference on historical memory with Czech, Slovak and Hungarian counterparts. The V4 is dead as a security or political format, but cultural and historical cooperation survives. The conference may serve Mr Nawrocki's stated interest in reviving the V4: the institute's engagement with Hungarian counterparts on memory politics may be advancing his agenda rather than the government's.\n\nThe larger questions from last week remain dark. The April 28-30 no-confidence vote against the climate minister — the most immediate test of coalition cohesion, flagged last week as carrying real strain — falls just outside this cycle's date range, and no preparatory coverage reached this desk. The Confederation diplomatic fallout and the Constitutional Tribunal oath standoff likewise produced no new evidence. Those threads carry forward unresolved.",
